      "id": "chapter-5",
      "number": 5,
      "title": "Method Two: AI ghostwriting",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The business model\nAI ghostwriting means producing written content for a business, creator, or entrepreneur who has expertise or a product but lacks the time or skill to publish consistently.",
        "Possible deliverables include:\n- Social media posts.\n- LinkedIn content.\n- Email newsletters.\n- Website copy.\n- Facebook advertisements.\nThe key is not simply generating text. Clients pay for writing that sounds like them, reflects their business, addresses their audience, and communicates useful ideas.\nStep 1: Choose one writing service and one niche\nStart narrow. Choose one deliverable and one market.",
        "Examples from the source include:\n- Deliverable: social media posts, newsletters, website text, or Facebook ads.\n- Niche: finance, fitness, technology startups, or real estate.\nA focused offer is easier to explain and improve than “I write anything for anyone.”",
        "A useful positioning statement:\nI help [specific type of client] publish [specific type of content] that helps them [business purpose].",
        "For example: “I help independent fitness coaches turn their expertise into a weekly email newsletter.”",
        "Do not claim specialist expertise you do not have. Learn the vocabulary, customer concerns, and commercial context of the niche before selling yourself as an expert.\nStep 2: Build a dedicated AI workspace for each client\nThe source demonstrates using Claude Projects. The underlying idea is to create a separate workspace containing reference material and instructions for one client.",
        "Collect material the client has permission to share or that is legitimately public, such as:\n- Previous social posts.\n- Earlier newsletters.\n- Podcast appearances and interviews.\n- Website pages.\n- Product descriptions.\n- Audience questions.\n- Brand guidelines and preferred terminology.\nOrganise the material so that the AI can understand the client’s background and communication style. Avoid uploading confidential or personal information unless the client has authorised it and the tool’s data terms are suitable.\nStep 3: Write detailed project instructions\nInstructions should define the output, audience, voice, and boundaries. Include:\n1. Client context: what the person or business does.\n2. Audience: who the content is for and what they care about.\n3. Voice: sentence length, vocabulary, formality, humour, and point of view.\n4. Content goals: educate, build trust, generate enquiries, retain subscribers, or support a launch.\n5. Style constraints: phrases to avoid, formatting preferences, and how promotional the writing should be.\n6. Accuracy rules: do not invent personal experiences, results, customer stories, statistics, or claims.\n7. Output format: length, structure, number of options, and any call to action.\nThe goal is not to imitate superficial quirks. It is to create writing that is faithful to the client’s real views and approved communication style.\nStep 4: Research what is happening in the niche\nThe source recommends a research tool such as Perplexity to find relevant weekly industry stories. For example, a writer serving the electric-vehicle industry might research the week’s major developments.",
        "A practical research process:\n1. Define the niche and the period being researched.\n2. Ask for the most important developments, not just a long list of headlines.\n3. Open the original sources.\n4. Verify dates, facts, figures, and quotations.\n5. Identify why each development matters to the client’s audience.\n6. Save source links alongside the notes.\n7. Exclude stories that are irrelevant, weakly sourced, or too speculative.\nResearch tools can help discover material, but the writer remains responsible for checking it.\nStep 5: Draft, edit, and approve\nUse the research and client workspace to generate drafts. Then apply the missing human judgment:\n- Is the angle genuinely interesting?\n- Does the opening give the reader a reason to continue?\n- Is the content specific rather than generic?\n- Does it sound consistent with the client?\n- Are all factual claims supported?\n- Is the call to action appropriate?\n- Would the client actually say this?\nRemove generic AI phrasing, empty motivational language, unsupported certainty, and repetitive structures. Do not publish invented first-person stories as if the client experienced them.",
        "For a new client, establish an approval process. Send drafts for review before publishing until the client has explicitly delegated that responsibility.\nStep 6: Package the service\nStart with a manageable deliverable, such as a defined number of posts or one newsletter per week. Agree on:\n- Deliverables and format.\n- Research responsibilities.\n- Number of revisions.\n- Turnaround time.\n- Approval process.\n- Publishing or scheduling responsibilities.\n- Payment schedule.\n- Ownership and permitted use of the finished content.\nThe source mentions Typefully for social scheduling and Kit for email newsletters as possible additions to the tool stack. Choose tools based on the actual service rather than adding subscriptions unnecessarily.\nWhat remains your job\nThe source estimates a one-to-two-week learning curve, 75% AI involvement, and $20–$40 per month in AI subscriptions. The most important human contribution is taste: selecting the right angle, knowing what is worth saying, editing generic output, and understanding the client.\nCommon failure points\n- Generic writing: use real client material and detailed instructions.\n- Outdated research: verify publication dates and original sources.\n- Invented claims: require evidence for statistics, outcomes, and personal stories.\n- Voice mismatch: compare drafts with approved examples and request client feedback.\n- Too many niches: specialise long enough to build genuine understanding.\n- Unclear scope: define revisions, research, and publishing responsibilities before work begins.\nBest fit: someone who reads widely, notices strong writing, and is willing to develop editorial judgment rather than relying on prompts alone."
      ]
