      "id": "chapter-6",
      "number": 6,
      "title": "Method Three: AI-built landing pages",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The business model\nA landing page is a focused, usually single-page website designed to persuade a visitor to take one primary action. That action might be buying a product, booking a consultation, joining a programme, or requesting a quote.",
        "The source identifies course creators, coaches, agencies, and software companies as potential customers. These businesses may already attract attention through content or advertising but need a page that turns some of that attention into enquiries or sales.",
        "The service is not merely “make a pretty website.” The value is a clear page that explains an offer and helps the right visitor act.\nStep 1: Learn one AI website builder\nThe source names Wix and Hostinger as beginner-friendly examples. Tool choice matters less than learning one builder well enough to create, edit, preview, and publish a page.",
        "Begin with a fictional practice project. For example:\nCreate a clean, modern landing page for a personal trainer selling an online coaching programme. Use a strong headline, explain who the programme is for, show the offer clearly, and include one prominent call to action.",
        "Treat the generated page as a first draft. Review:\n- Headline clarity.\n- Offer explanation.\n- Page structure.\n- Image relevance.\n- Mobile layout.\n- Readability.\n- Call-to-action placement.\n- Form or booking functionality.\nA generated page can look polished while still being commercially weak.\nStep 2: Understand the basic conversion structure\nThe source highlights three central elements: headline, proof, and one clear call to action. A practical landing page can organise these elements as follows:\n| Section | Purpose | Questions to answer |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Headline | Communicate the main value | What is offered, and for whom? |\n| Supporting statement | Make the promise understandable | What does the customer get? |\n| Problem and solution | Connect the offer to a need | What difficulty does it address? |\n| Benefits or deliverables | Explain what is included | What will the buyer receive? |\n| Proof | Reduce uncertainty | Are there genuine testimonials, examples, credentials, or demonstrations? |\n| Process or offer details | Explain what happens next | How does it work, and what does it cost if disclosed? |\n| Call to action | Prompt one next step | Book, buy, enquire, or sign up? |\n| FAQ or reassurance | Address real objections | What do visitors commonly need to know? |",
        "Use genuine proof only. Never fabricate testimonials, client logos, results, reviews, or credentials.\nStep 3: Find businesses with a clear use case\nLook for businesses that already have something to sell and a reason to send traffic to a focused page. Potential signs include:\n- They promote a service through social media but have no clear destination.\n- Their website makes visitors navigate through several pages to enquire.\n- Their offer is difficult to understand.\n- Their booking or enquiry process is confusing.\n- They are running a campaign that needs a dedicated page.\nDo not promise a specific conversion-rate improvement without evidence. Your first offer should be to build a clear, functional page—not to guarantee revenue.\nStep 4: Build and deliver the page\nA client workflow:\n1. Hold a discovery conversation.\n2. Confirm the target audience and single page objective.\n3. Collect approved offer details, images, brand assets, and proof.\n4. Draft the page structure and copy.\n5. Generate the initial design in the builder.\n6. Edit the copy and layout.\n7. Test desktop and mobile views.\n8. Test forms, booking links, buttons, and tracking if included.\n9. Obtain client approval.\n10. Publish using an agreed domain and access arrangement.\nMake clear who owns the website account, domain, copy, images, and ongoing subscription.\nStep 5: Offer ongoing optimisation\nThe source suggests charging per page initially and then offering a monthly fee to keep improving it. A sensible ongoing service might include:\n- Reviewing visitor behaviour where analytics are available.\n- Identifying confusing sections.\n- Testing alternative headlines or calls to action.\n- Updating offer details.\n- Checking forms and links.\n- Reporting changes and results.\nSome builders offer testing features, but availability and functionality vary. Do not promise automatic optimisation unless the selected tool genuinely supports the required tests and the traffic is sufficient to interpret them.\nWhat remains your job\nThe source estimates two to four weeks to learn, around 80% AI involvement, and approximately $30 per month for a builder. AI may draft copy and generate layouts, but you must understand the offer, assess conversion fundamentals, communicate with clients, and test the finished page.\nCommon failure points\n- Beautiful but unclear page: make the offer and audience obvious.\n- Too many calls to action: focus on one primary action.\n- Fake or weak proof: use only verifiable material.\n- Broken forms or buttons: test every interaction before launch.\n- Poor mobile layout: review the page on mobile, not only in the desktop editor.\n- Unrealistic promises: sell the deliverable and optimisation process, not guaranteed sales.\nBest fit: someone who enjoys visual building and wants to sell a concrete business deliverable."
      ]
