      "title": "Method Four: AI-assisted Shopify commerce",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The business model\nThis model uses AI and e-commerce automation to launch an online store. The source describes a workflow in which an AI store builder helps create the shop, while a product and fulfilment platform such as AutoDS helps import products, update listings, and route orders to suppliers.",
        "The attraction is that the operator may not need to hold inventory or pack every order personally. But “no inventory” does not mean “no responsibility.” The store owner remains responsible for the customer experience and for the promises made on the storefront.\nStep 1: Choose a market before building the store\nThe source describes choosing a niche such as fashion, electronics, or pets. Do not select a niche only because a tool offers it as a dropdown option.",
        "Evaluate:\n- Whether a defined audience wants the products.\n- Whether the products solve a recognisable need or have clear appeal.\n- Whether suppliers are dependable.\n- Whether delivery times are acceptable.\n- Whether product quality can be checked.\n- Whether the selling price leaves room for all costs.\n- Whether you can compete without making misleading claims.\nStart with a narrow product category rather than a large catalogue of unrelated items.\nStep 2: Use an AI store builder for the initial setup\nThe source demonstrates a guided store-building workflow using a service called Build Your Store. The tool asks for basic details and a niche, then creates an initial Shopify store.",
        "The general process is:\n1. Create an account with the selected store-building tool.\n2. Enter the requested business details.\n3. Choose a niche.\n4. Follow the setup prompts.\n5. Review the generated storefront.\n6. Edit the brand, navigation, product categories, copy, and design.\n7. Check the mobile storefront.\n8. Confirm that policies, contact details, and checkout settings are accurate.\nAn automatically generated store is a starting point. It is not proof that the products are good, that the business is legally ready, or that customers will buy.\nStep 3: Connect products and fulfilment\nThe source describes connecting AutoDS to Shopify. It presents the tool as a way to import products, update prices and images, and send orders to suppliers for fulfilment.",
        "Before importing products, verify:\n- Supplier identity and reliability.\n- Product specifications and variations.\n- Whether images accurately represent the item.\n- Stock availability and update frequency.\n- Delivery estimates and tracking.\n- Return, refund, and replacement procedures.\n- Total landed cost and your selling margin.\n- Whether the supplier and product comply with the rules in the markets where you sell.\nAutomation can pass an order to a supplier; it cannot guarantee that the supplier will deliver a satisfactory product on time.\nStep 4: Calculate the real unit economics\nDo not judge the store by revenue alone. For each product, estimate:",
        "Contribution per order = selling price − product cost − shipping − payment fees − platform or app costs allocated per order − advertising cost per order − expected refunds, replacements, and chargebacks.",
        "The exact costs depend on your setup. Include taxes and other obligations relevant to your jurisdiction. A product that appears profitable before advertising, refunds, and payment fees may lose money after those costs.",
        "The source mentions a promotional example with a free store-building tool, a Shopify trial, and a 99-cent initial AutoDS offer. Those are time-sensitive promotional terms, not a reliable estimate of the full cost of operating a store. Check current pricing, trial conditions, subscription renewals, transaction fees, and paid app requirements.\nStep 5: Create a customer acquisition plan\nA store needs visitors. The source identifies sending people to the store as the operator’s remaining task but does not provide a detailed traffic strategy.",
        "At minimum, decide how the store will reach its audience. Do not assume that publishing a store automatically creates demand. Test a small number of products and messages before expanding the catalogue or committing to significant advertising spend.\nStep 6: Operate the store responsibly\nEven with automated fulfilment, monitor:\n- Orders and payment status.\n- Supplier stock and price changes.\n- Dispatch and delivery progress.\n- Customer questions.\n- Refund and return requests.\n- Product complaints.\n- Store errors and broken checkout flows.\nPublish honest delivery estimates and clear return policies. Make sure the customer knows who they are buying from and how to obtain support.\nWhat remains your job\nThe source estimates that AI may handle 95% of the setup and routine workflow, with a very low initial learning curve. It describes choosing a niche and bringing visitors as the operator’s main tasks. In practice, product selection, supplier oversight, customer service, compliance, cash flow, and quality control remain substantial responsibilities.\nCommon failure points\n- Choosing products only because an AI tool labels them winners: validate demand and supplier quality.\n- Ignoring the full cost structure: calculate contribution after fees, shipping, advertising, and refunds.\n- Assuming fulfilment automation removes customer service: the store owner still owns the customer relationship.\n- Misleading product images or claims: verify product details and use accurate descriptions.\n- Scaling before testing: validate a small number of products before expanding.\nBest fit: someone interested in commerce who is prepared to manage operations and customer responsibility, not just build a storefront."
      ]
